--- CHUNK 22 ---
A Generative Foundation Model for Multimodal Histopathology
Results
Ablation study of model design
Alignment Loss Design. Beyond the attention architecture, we investigated the effect of the spatial alignment and semantic regularisation objectives used during MUPAD pretraining. We compared three variants: (i) Naive denoising loss (SiT 26 ): the standard velocity-prediction flow-matching objective with no additional alignment term; (ii) REPA 48 : an MLP-based alignment strategy that projects intermediate denoiser representations onto a pretrained encoder 28 feature space via a lightweight multi-layer perceptron; (iii) MUPAD alignment : our proposed CNN-based spatial alignment with an auxiliary CLS token denoising loss that enforces global semantic consistency between noisy intermediate representations and the clean image's semantic embedding throughout the denoising trajectory. The rationale for MUPAD's design is twofold: the CNN spatial alignment preserves the fine-grained histological texture information critical for high-fidelity pathology image synthesis, while the CLS token denoising loss anchors the global semantic content of intermediate noisy representations to the clean-image manifold.
We evaluated convergence on the image-to-image generation task, plotting image similarity and FID against training steps in Fig. 7b. MUPAD improves steadily and monotonically from an image similarity of 0.259 at 20K steps to 0.424 at 100K steps-nearly twice the similarity achieved by both REPA (0.243) and SiT (0.242), which remain largely stagnant throughout training. FID scores are consistent with this trend: MUPAD reaches 156.39 at 100K steps, outperforming REPA (187.39) and SiT (183.22) by 16.5% and 14.6%, respectively. Notably, REPA does not confer a meaningful advantage over the naive SiT baseline in our setting, suggesting that MLP-based global alignment is insufficient for capturing the fine-grained spatial structure of histological images. We also provide visual examples illustrating model convergence at representative training checkpoints (Fig. 7c). Taken together, these results demonstrate that the combination of CNN spatial alignment and the CLS token denoising loss provides training signals that substantially accelerate convergence and improve both semantic fidelity and generation quality relative to existing alignment strategies.

--- CHUNK 23 ---
A Generative Foundation Model for Multimodal Histopathology
Results
Ablation study of model design
Figure 7: Ablation studies of MUPAD. (a) Comparison of the proposed decoupled cross-attention (DCA) against shared cross-attention across multimodal embeddings. DCA consistently improves performance across image-to-image, text-to-image, and RNA-to-image generation tasks , with relative FID reductions of 13.6%, 7.9%, and 12.4%, respectively. (b) FID and image similarity trajectories for image-to-image generation of SiT, REPA, and MUPAD. Lower FID and higher image similarity indicate better performance. (c) Visual comparison of image-to-image generation across training steps (20K-100K) for MUPAD, REPA 48 , and SiT 26 .

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel experimental results figure combining bar plots, line plots, and microscopy images.

Panel (a) shows paired bar charts comparing “Shared CA (baseline)” versus “DCA (MUPAD)” across Image-to-Image, Text-to-Image, and RNA-to-Image tasks. Left y-axis is Image Similarity, where higher is marked better; right y-axis is FID, where lower is marked better. DCA/MUPAD visibly improves similarity and lowers FID across all three tasks.

Panel (b) shows training-step curves for MUPAD, REPA, and SiT from 20k to 100k steps. Left plot measures Image Similarity, with MUPAD increasing strongly over training while REPA and SiT remain nearly flat. Right plot measures FID, with all methods improving, but MUPAD reaches the lowest FID by 100k steps.

Panel (c) shows histology/microscopy image samples: a reference tissue image on the left and generated image grids across training steps 20k, 40k, 60k, 80k, and 100k for MUPAD, REPA, and SiT. MUPAD images visibly become more similar to the reference tissue texture and morphology over training, while REPA and SiT appear less detailed and more washed out.
